(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["serving",{"c:gpu-memory-maths-for-llms":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>GPU memory for serving is \u003cb>weights + KV cache + runtime overhead\u003c/b>. Weights are fixed: parameters times bytes per parameter. The KV cache grows with every token of every concurrent request. On a serving GPU, whatever is left after the weights becomes the KV pool, and the size of that pool decides how many users you can serve at once.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The budget\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>weights      = parameters × bytes per parameter   (BF16: 2, FP8: 1, 4-bit formats: about 0.55 to 0.65 in practice)\nKV per token = 2 × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes per value        (the 2 is one K and one V)\nKV total     = KV per token × tokens per sequence × concurrent sequences\noverhead     = activations, CUDA graphs and framework buffers: plan 1 to 3 GB per GPU\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The Inference mechanics module derived the KV formula. Here you use it to size hardware.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: Qwen3-8B, a real config\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Qwen3-8B's \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code> on Hugging Face gives \u003ccode>num_hidden_layers: 36\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>num_attention_heads: 32\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>num_key_value_heads: 8\u003c/code> (grouped-query attention), \u003ccode>head_dim: 128\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>torch_dtype: bfloat16\u003c/code>. The model has 8,190,735,360 parameters and a native context of 32,768 tokens.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>weights, BF16  = 8.19e9 × 2 bytes                 = 16.4 GB  (the published files total 16.38 GB)\nKV per token   = 2 × 36 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes       = 147,456 bytes = 144 KiB\nKV, 32k tokens = 147,456 × 32,768                 = 4.83 GB\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Real quantised files are bigger than \"parameters × 0.5\", because scales add bits and some layers (embeddings, output head) stay at higher precision. The official Qwen3-8B files: FP8 9.44 GB, AWQ 4-bit 6.10 GB, GGUF Q4_K_M 5.03 GB (about 4.9 bits per weight).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>One user, 32k context\u003c/th>\u003cth>16-bit (BF16)\u003c/th>\u003cth>4-bit (Q4_K_M)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Weights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16.4 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5.0 GB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>KV cache at 16-bit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.8 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.8 GB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Overhead\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 to 2 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 to 2 GB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Total\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>about 22 to 23 GB\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>about 11 to 12 GB\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fits on\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A 24 GB GPU only just; 32 GB or more is comfortable\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A 12 to 16 GB GPU or a 16 GB laptop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Quantising the weights does not shrink the KV cache. Store the cache at 8 bits as well and the 32k context needs 2.4 GB instead of 4.8 GB.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>From memory to concurrency\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On a server you care about the pool, not one user. An 80 GB H100 running vLLM with its default \u003ccode>--gpu-memory-utilization 0.92\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>usable       = 80 × 0.92                      = 73.6 GB\nKV pool      = 73.6 - 16.4 (weights) - 3 (overhead) ≈ 54 GB\ntokens held  = 54e9 / 147,456                 ≈ 368,000 tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Average tokens per sequence (prompt plus output)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Concurrent sequences\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2,048\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 180\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8,192\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 45\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>32,768\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 11\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Long contexts eat concurrency: the same GPU serves 16 times fewer users at 32k tokens than at 2k. vLLM prints its own version of this at start-up, for example \u003ccode>GPU KV cache size: 643,232 tokens\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Maximum concurrency for 40,960 tokens per request: 15.70x\u003c/code>. Compare it with your estimate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A bigger model makes the pool shrink fast. Qwen3-32B has 64 layers, 8 KV heads and head_dim 128, so 256 KiB per token. In BF16 its 65.5 GB of weights leave only about 5 GB of KV on an 80 GB GPU (about 19,000 tokens), which is barely usable. In FP8 (32.8 GB) the pool is about 38 GB, or roughly 144,000 tokens, and about 288,000 with an FP8 KV cache.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mixture-of-experts models\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For MoE models, \u003cb>memory follows total parameters, speed follows active parameters\u003c/b>. gpt-oss-120b has 117B parameters, of which 5.1B are active per token. Its MoE weights are stored in MXFP4, so the checkpoint is about 65 GB and the model card says it fits on a single 80 GB GPU. It is also frugal with KV: head_dim 64, 8 KV heads, and half of its 36 layers use a 128-token sliding window, so only the 18 full-attention layers grow with context, about 36 KiB per token.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A sizing helper\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def gpu_budget(params_b, bytes_per_param, layers, kv_heads, head_dim, kv_bytes=2,\n               gpu_gb=80, util=0.92, overhead_gb=3, avg_tokens=4096):\n    weights = params_b * bytes_per_param\n    kv_per_token = 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * kv_bytes\n    pool_gb = gpu_gb * util - weights - overhead_gb\n    tokens = pool_gb * 1e9 / kv_per_token\n    return round(weights, 1), kv_per_token, round(tokens), round(tokens / avg_tokens)\n\nprint(gpu_budget(8.19, 2, 36, 8, 128))    # (16.4, 147456, 367703, 90)  Qwen3-8B BF16, H100\nprint(gpu_budget(32.76, 1, 64, 8, 128))   # (32.8, 262144, 144348, 35)  Qwen3-32B FP8, H100\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Read the config, not the marketing.\u003c/b> Layers, KV heads and head_dim decide the cache. Models with multi-head latent attention (DeepSeek) or hybrid linear attention keep far smaller caches than this formula suggests; trust the engine's start-up numbers for those.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Set the maximum model length to what you need\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>--max-model-len\u003c/code> in vLLM). The engine must be able to hold at least one maximum-length request, and a needlessly long limit invites a few giant requests to crowd out everyone else.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quantise the cache\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>--kv-cache-dtype fp8\u003c/code>) to double capacity, then check long-context quality on your eval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Leave headroom.\u003c/b> Running out of KV memory at peak forces the engine to pause and recompute requests, which hurts latency far more than slightly lower concurrency.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Estimate the memory needed for an 8B model at 16-bit and at 4-bit with a 32k context.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Using Qwen3-8B (36 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128): weights are about 16.4 GB in BF16 and about 5 GB in a 4-bit GGUF (Q4_K_M); the KV cache is 2 × 36 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 144 KiB per token, so 32,768 tokens need about 4.8 GB at 16-bit in both cases; add 1 to 2 GB of overhead. Total: roughly 22 to 23 GB at 16-bit (a tight fit on a 24 GB GPU) and 11 to 12 GB at 4-bit. An 8-bit KV cache would save another 2.4 GB.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a 4-bit model still need almost 5 GB for its 32k context?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Weight quantisation only shrinks the weights. The KV cache is stored separately, at 16-bit by default, and its size depends on layers, KV heads, head dimension and tokens, not on how the weights are stored.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Roughly how many 8,192-token conversations can Qwen3-8B in BF16 hold on one 80 GB GPU, and what would double it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 54 GB of KV pool divided by 8,192 × 144 KiB (about 1.2 GB per conversation) gives about 45. Storing the KV cache in FP8, or the weights in FP8 (which frees about 7 GB more pool), raises it; FP8 KV alone roughly doubles it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:local-inference-with-llama-cpp-and-ollama":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>llama.cpp\u003c/b> is the C/C++ engine that runs GGUF model files on almost anything: Apple silicon, NVIDIA and AMD GPUs, plain CPUs, or a mix. \u003cb>Ollama\u003c/b> wraps local inference in a model manager with an OpenAI-compatible server. For a single user, generation speed is roughly memory bandwidth divided by model size, which is why 4-bit files run fastest and why the same model is several times faster on a GPU than on a CPU.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The local toolbox\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use it for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>llama.cpp\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Engine plus CLI tools: \u003ccode>llama-cli\u003c/code> (chat), \u003ccode>llama-server\u003c/code> (OpenAI-compatible API and web UI), \u003ccode>llama-bench\u003c/code> (speed tests)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full control, any hardware, scripting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ollama\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model manager and server built for convenience; \u003ccode>ollama run\u003c/code>, API on port 11434\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Easiest setup on a developer machine\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LM Studio\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Desktop app with a chat UI and local server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Non-developers, quick comparisons\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MLX\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Apple's array framework with LLM tooling\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast inference and fine-tuning on Macs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>GGUF\u003c/b> is llama.cpp's single-file format: weights plus tokenizer, chat template and metadata. A repository usually offers several quantisations of one model, named by type: \u003ccode>Q4_K_M\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Q5_K_M\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Q6_K\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Q8_0\u003c/code> and so on (the next lesson explains them).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Run a model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With llama.cpp (install with \u003ccode>brew install llama.cpp\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>winget install llama.cpp\u003c/code>, prebuilt binaries or a source build):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># Download Qwen3-8B at Q4_K_M from Hugging Face and serve it with an OpenAI-compatible API\nllama-server -hf Qwen/Qwen3-8B-GGUF:Q4_K_M -c 16384 --port 8080\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>-c\u003c/code> sets the context size and \u003ccode>--port\u003c/code> the port (set it explicitly; defaults have changed between releases). Recent releases also ship a combined \u003ccode>llama\u003c/code> command (\u003ccode>llama serve -hf ...\u003c/code>). Any OpenAI client then works with \u003ccode>base_url=\"http://localhost:8080/v1\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>With Ollama:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>ollama run qwen3:8b --verbose        # the default tag is a Q4_K_M build, about 5.2 GB\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Measure tokens per second\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are two speeds, and they differ by an order of magnitude or more:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prompt processing (prefill)\u003c/b>: how fast the input is read, in parallel.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generation (decode)\u003c/b>: how fast new tokens appear, one at a time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ollama run --verbose\u003c/code> prints both after each answer as \u003ccode>prompt eval rate\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>eval rate\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>llama-bench\u003c/code> measures them properly, averaging five repetitions by default:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>llama-bench -hf Qwen/Qwen3-8B-GGUF:Q4_K_M            # tests pp512 (prefill) and tg128 (generation)\nllama-bench -hf Qwen/Qwen3-8B-GGUF:Q4_K_M -d 0,8192  # repeat at 8k tokens of existing context\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The llama-bench documentation's own sample run (a 7B model at Q4_K_M on a CUDA GPU) shows about 7,340 tokens per second for \u003ccode>pp512\u003c/code> and about 121 for \u003ccode>tg128\u003c/code>: prefill is roughly 60 times faster per token.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Predict the number before you measure it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a dense model and one user, every generated token reads all the weights once:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>generation tokens/s  ≤  memory bandwidth (bytes/s) / model size (bytes)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Hardware (memory bandwidth)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Qwen3-8B Q4_K_M (5.03 GB)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Qwen3-8B Q8_0 (8.71 GB)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Laptop with 120 GB/s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most 24 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most 14 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>GPU or Apple chip with 400 GB/s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most 80 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most 46 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data-centre GPU, H100 (3.35 TB/s)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most 666 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most 385 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Real speeds land noticeably below this ceiling; reaching 60 to 80% of it is good. The table explains three everyday observations: 4-bit runs faster than 8-bit; a model that spills from GPU memory into system RAM slows dramatically (system RAM is much slower); and MoE models run surprisingly well locally, because only the active experts are read for each token (gpt-oss-20b reads about 3.6B active parameters per token, not 21B).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Default to Q4_K_M\u003c/b>; use Q5_K_M, Q6_K or Q8_0 if memory allows and quality matters; avoid 2 and 3-bit types for serious work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Watch the context default.\u003c/b> Ollama uses a 4,096-token context unless you set \u003ccode>OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>num_ctx\u003c/code>; longer prompts get truncated. In llama-server set \u003ccode>-c\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Partial offload.\u003c/b> If the model does not fit in VRAM, \u003ccode>-ngl\u003c/code> (GPU layers) puts some layers on the GPU and the rest on the CPU; expect a big slowdown.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parallel requests multiply memory.\u003c/b> Ollama's \u003ccode>OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL\u003c/code> defaults to 1, and required memory scales with parallel requests times context length. For many concurrent users, use vLLM or SGLang instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quantise the cache too\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0\u003c/code> in Ollama (needs Flash Attention) or \u003ccode>-ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0\u003c/code> in llama.cpp.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Good fits: development, offline and air-gapped use, privacy-sensitive personal tools, edge devices, small internal services. Poor fits: high-concurrency production serving.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you run a GGUF model on a laptop and measure its tokens per second?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Install llama.cpp or Ollama, then run a quantised model, for example \u003ccode>llama-server -hf Qwen/Qwen3-8B-GGUF:Q4_K_M -c 16384 --port 8080\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>ollama run qwen3:8b --verbose\u003c/code>. Measure with \u003ccode>llama-bench -hf Qwen/Qwen3-8B-GGUF:Q4_K_M\u003c/code>, which reports prompt-processing speed (\u003ccode>pp512\u003c/code>) and generation speed (\u003ccode>tg128\u003c/code>) in tokens per second averaged over several runs, or read \u003ccode>prompt eval rate\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>eval rate\u003c/code> from Ollama's verbose output. Report both numbers, and compare generation speed with the ceiling of memory bandwidth divided by file size.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your laptop has 100 GB/s of memory bandwidth. Roughly what generation speed should you expect from an 8B model at Q4_K_M (5 GB) and at Q8_0 (8.7 GB)?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The ceilings are 100 / 5 = 20 tokens per second and 100 / 8.7 ≈ 11.5 tokens per second; real speeds will be somewhat lower, perhaps 13 to 16 and 7 to 9.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is prompt processing so much faster per token than generation?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prefill handles all prompt tokens in one batched pass, so the weights are read once for hundreds of tokens and the GPU's compute is the limit. Generation produces one token per pass and must re-read all the weights for each token, so memory bandwidth is the limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:quantisation-formats-gguf-awq-gptq-and-fp8":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Quantisation stores numbers in fewer bits, usually as small codes plus a shared \u003cb>scale\u003c/b> per block of values. \u003cb>Weight-only\u003c/b> formats (4-bit GGUF, AWQ, GPTQ) shrink memory and speed up memory-bound generation. \u003cb>Weight-and-activation\u003c/b> formats (FP8, and FP4 on the newest GPUs) also use faster low-precision maths, which speeds up prefill and large batches. Formats differ in where they run and how cleverly they pick scales; every one must be checked on your own eval.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How block quantisation works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take a block of four weights and store them as 4-bit signed integers (codes from -7 to 7) plus one scale:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>weights   [0.12, -0.50, 0.33, 0.07]\nscale   = max |w| / 7 = 0.50 / 7 = 0.0714\ncodes   = round(w / scale) = [2, -7, 5, 1]\ndecoded = codes × scale   = [0.143, -0.500, 0.357, 0.071]    small errors\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Now put one outlier in the block:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>weights   [0.12, -0.50, 0.33, 4.00]\nscale   = 4.00 / 7 = 0.571\ncodes   = [0, -1, 1, 7]\ndecoded = [0.000, -0.571, 0.571, 4.000]   the small weights are destroyed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Outliers are the central problem, and each method attacks it differently: smaller blocks (each outlier spoils fewer neighbours), better scale formats, protecting important channels, compensating errors, and keeping sensitive layers (embeddings, the output head) at higher precision. Scales cost bits too: a 16-bit scale per 32 weights adds half a bit, which is why \"4-bit\" files work out at 4.5 to 5 bits per weight.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The formats you will meet\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Format\u003c/th>\u003cth>Bits per weight\u003c/th>\u003cth>Quantises\u003c/th>\u003cth>How scales are chosen\u003c/th>\u003cth>Runs on\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>GGUF k-quants (\u003ccode>Q4_K_M\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Q5_K_M\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Q6_K\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Q8_0\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Q4_K_M about 4.9, Q8_0 about 8.5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Blocks grouped into super-blocks with their own scales; \"M\" variants keep some tensors at higher precision\u003c/td>\u003ctd>llama.cpp, Ollama, LM Studio: CPU, Apple, consumer and data-centre GPUs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>GPTQ\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4 (also 3 or 8) plus scales\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One-shot, layer by layer, using approximate second-order information to compensate rounding errors on calibration data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GPU servers: vLLM, SGLang, Transformers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>AWQ\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4 plus scales\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Finds the roughly 1% of salient channels from activation statistics and scales them up before rounding\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GPU servers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>FP8 (E4M3, often \"W8A8\")\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights and activations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per-tensor, per-channel or per-block scales; often no calibration needed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>NVIDIA Ada, Hopper and Blackwell, recent AMD\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MXFP4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.25\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights (often only MoE experts)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4-bit floats (E2M1) sharing a power-of-two scale per 32 values\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Native on Blackwell; kernels exist for older GPUs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>NVFP4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weights and activations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4-bit floats with an FP8 scale per 16 values plus one FP32 scale per tensor\u003c/td>\u003ctd>NVIDIA Blackwell\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For Qwen3-8B (8.19B parameters) the published files show the trade-off directly: BF16 16.38 GB, FP8 9.44 GB, GGUF Q8_0 8.71 GB, AWQ 6.10 GB, Q5_K_M 5.85 GB, Q4_K_M 5.03 GB. gpt-oss ships its MoE weights in MXFP4, which is how a 117B-parameter model fits on one 80 GB GPU. NVIDIA reports that NVFP4 is about 3.5 times smaller than FP16 and within about 1% of FP8 accuracy on DeepSeek-R1-0528 benchmarks.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Weight-only versus weight-and-activation\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Weight-only (W4A16)\u003c/b>: weights are stored in 4 bits but converted back to 16-bit for the matrix maths. Decode at small batch is memory-bound, so reading about 3 times fewer bytes makes it much faster. At large batches the maths becomes the bottleneck and the conversion overhead shows, so the advantage shrinks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Weight-and-activation (FP8, NVFP4)\u003c/b>: the multiply itself runs in low precision on tensor cores. H100's FP8 throughput is twice its BF16 throughput, which speeds up prefill and big batches as well. FP8 is the common default for GPU serving in late 2026 because it is close to lossless.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>KV cache quantisation\u003c/b> is separate (\u003ccode>--kv-cache-dtype fp8\u003c/code> in vLLM, \u003ccode>-ctk q8_0\u003c/code> in llama.cpp) and attacks the other memory consumer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quality: what to expect\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>8-bit (FP8, Q8_0): usually indistinguishable from 16-bit on typical tasks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>4-bit (AWQ, GPTQ, Q4_K_M, NVFP4): small losses on broad benchmarks, larger on small models and on hard maths, code, long-context and non-English tasks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>2 to 3-bit: noticeable degradation; for experiments or very constrained devices.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Tools: LLM Compressor (the vLLM project's library, which absorbed the now-deprecated AutoAWQ) for FP8, INT4 and FP4 checkpoints; GPTQModel; llama.cpp's \u003ccode>llama-quantize\u003c/code> for GGUF; NVIDIA Model Optimizer. Many labs publish official quantised versions, which are usually the safest choice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you compare the quality and speed of two quantised versions of a model, for example Qwen3-8B at Q4_K_M and Q8_0?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Speed: run \u003ccode>llama-bench\u003c/code> on both files on the target hardware and compare prompt processing (\u003ccode>pp512\u003c/code>) and generation (\u003ccode>tg128\u003c/code>); expect Q4_K_M to generate about 1.7 times faster on bandwidth-bound hardware because its file is 5.03 GB against 8.71 GB. Quality: serve both with identical settings (same prompts, chat template and temperature 0) and run the same eval of 100 or more task examples, scoring them identically; optionally also compare perplexity on in-domain text. Then pick the smallest format whose eval score stays within your tolerance of the larger one, and note any categories (maths, long context) where it slips.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a 4-bit weight-only model be slower than an FP8 model at high batch sizes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At high batch sizes serving becomes compute-bound. Weight-only formats still do their maths in 16-bit after converting weights on the fly, while FP8 runs the multiplies on FP8 tensor cores at twice the BF16 rate, so FP8 wins once bandwidth stops being the bottleneck.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the main difference between MXFP4 and NVFP4?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Both store 4-bit E2M1 values in blocks with a shared scale. MXFP4 uses blocks of 32 with a power-of-two (E8M0) scale, about 4.25 bits per value; NVFP4 uses blocks of 16 with a more precise FP8 (E4M3) scale plus a per-tensor FP32 scale, about 4.5 bits per value, which reduces quantisation error.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:pagedattention-and-vllm":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Early serving systems reserved one contiguous chunk of KV memory per request, sized for the longest possible output, and wasted most of it. \u003cb>PagedAttention\u003c/b> stores the KV cache in small fixed-size blocks allocated on demand, like pages of virtual memory, and finds them through a per-request block table. Waste falls to part of one block per request, many more requests fit in memory, batches grow, and throughput rises.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The problem: reservation and fragmentation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take Qwen3-8B on an 80 GB GPU with about 54 GB of KV pool (from the memory lesson). If every request reserves room for the full 32,768-token context, each one claims 4.83 GB, so only \u003cb>11\u003c/b> requests fit. If real requests average 2,000 tokens, about 6% of the reserved memory ever holds data. On top of that, freed chunks of different sizes fragment the memory. The vLLM team measured that existing systems wasted 60 to 80% of KV-cache memory to fragmentation and over-reservation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The fix: blocks and a block table\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The KV pool is divided into \u003cb>blocks\u003c/b> of a fixed number of tokens (16 is the usual size in vLLM).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Each request has a \u003cb>block table\u003c/b> that maps its logical blocks (first 16 tokens, next 16 tokens, ...) to physical blocks anywhere in GPU memory.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A new physical block is allocated only when the previous one fills up, so the only waste is the unfilled tail of each request's last block.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The attention kernel reads keys and values through the block table; that kernel is what \"PagedAttention\" names.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Request A, 35 tokens:  logical [0][1][2]  -&gt;  physical 7, 2, 9   (block 9 holds 3 of 16 tokens)\nRequest B, 20 tokens:  logical [0][1]     -&gt;  physical 4, 1      (block 1 holds 4 of 16 tokens)\n\nPhysical pool:  [0 free][1 B][2 A][3 free][4 B][5 free][6 free][7 A][8 free][9 A] ...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With the same 54 GB pool and 2,000-token requests, about 180 requests now fit instead of 11. The vLLM team reported memory waste under 4%, and the paper reports 2 to 4 times the throughput of the previous state-of-the-art systems (FasterTransformer and Orca) at the same latency, with bigger gains for longer sequences and larger models. Throughput does not rise as much as concurrency, because each decode step gets slower as the batch grows, but the GPU is no longer starved of work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Sharing blocks: copy-on-write\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Because blocks are reference-counted, several sequences can point at the same physical block:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parallel sampling\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>n &gt; 1\u003c/code>) and beam search share the prompt's blocks instead of copying them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Requests with the \u003cb>same prompt prefix\u003c/b> can share its blocks, which is the basis of prefix caching (two lessons on).\u003c/li>\u003cli>When a shared block must change, it is copied first (copy-on-write), exactly as an operating system handles forked processes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When memory runs out: preemption\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sequences grow by one token per step, so a batch that fitted can stop fitting. vLLM then \u003cb>preempts\u003c/b> some requests: in its current engine the default is to free their blocks and recompute them later. It logs a warning, and the \u003ccode>vllm:num_preemptions\u003c/code> metric counts these events. Frequent preemption ruins latency. The fixes vLLM's tuning guide lists: raise \u003ccode>--gpu-memory-utilization\u003c/code>, lower \u003ccode>--max-num-seqs\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--max-num-batched-tokens\u003c/code>, or add GPUs with tensor or pipeline parallelism. A smaller \u003ccode>--max-model-len\u003c/code> and an FP8 KV cache help too.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>PagedAttention made vLLM, from a UC Berkeley team in 2023, the default open serving engine, and the idea is now standard: SGLang stores its cache in a paged layout with a page size of one token, and TensorRT-LLM and other engines use paged KV caches too. As a user, the levers are the flags above, and the start-up log line \u003ccode>GPU KV cache size: N tokens\u003c/code> tells you what you got.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain how paging the KV cache raises throughput.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decode throughput depends on how many sequences you can batch, which is limited by KV-cache memory. Without paging, each request reserves contiguous memory for its maximum length, so most of the pool sits reserved but empty and fragmented. Paging allocates small fixed-size blocks on demand and maps them through a block table, so waste is limited to the last partial block per request, and blocks can be shared between sequences with the same prefix. Far more requests fit at once, the engine batches more of them per decode step, and tokens per second per GPU rise (the paper reports 2 to 4 times over earlier systems).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In this lesson's example, how many requests fit with full-length reservation and with paging, and why is the throughput gain smaller than that ratio?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 11 with reservation (4.83 GB each) and about 180 with paging (2,000-token requests). Throughput rises less than 16 times because each decode step reads more KV data as the batch grows, so per-step time increases and per-user speed falls; there are also compute limits at large batches.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is preemption in vLLM, and what would you change if you saw it often?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the growing KV cache no longer fits, the engine pauses some requests, frees their blocks and recomputes them later, which adds latency. Raise \u003ccode>--gpu-memory-utilization\u003c/code>, lower \u003ccode>--max-num-seqs\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--max-model-len\u003c/code>, use an FP8 KV cache or FP8 weights, or add GPUs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:continuous-batching":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Generating a token for one sequence reads all the model's weights; generating a token for 32 sequences in the same step reads them once and costs only a little more. So servers batch. \u003cb>Static batching\u003c/b> waits for the slowest sequence in a batch before starting the next; \u003cb>continuous batching\u003c/b> (iteration-level scheduling) lets sequences join and leave at every decode step, keeping the GPU full. It is the main reason one GPU can serve hundreds of users.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why batching is nearly free during decode\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Decode is memory-bound: each step must read the weights, plus each sequence's KV cache. A bandwidth-only estimate for Qwen3-8B in BF16 on an H100 (3.35 TB/s), with every sequence holding 2,000 tokens of context:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Batch size\u003c/th>\u003cth>Step time (ceiling)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens/s per user\u003c/th>\u003cth>Total tokens/s\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5.0 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>201\u003c/td>\u003ctd>201\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5.6 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>179\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,430\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>32\u003c/td>\u003ctd>7.7 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>130\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,152\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>64\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.5 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>95\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6,082\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>128\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16.2 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>62\u003c/td>\u003ctd>7,922\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>step time ≈ (weight bytes + batch × KV bytes per sequence) / memory bandwidth\n          = (16.4 GB + 32 × 2,000 × 147,456 B) / 3.35 TB/s ≈ 7.7 ms at batch 32\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These are ceilings; real engines reach perhaps half to two-thirds of them. The shape is what matters: total throughput climbs steeply while per-user speed falls slowly. You choose the operating point from a latency target, for example \"at least 30 tokens per second per user\", which is a time per output token (TPOT) of about 33 ms.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Static versus continuous batching\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Static batching: eight requests start together and need 50, 80, 120, 200, 300, 450, 700 and 1,000 output tokens. The batch runs for 1,000 steps with 8 slots, which is 8,000 slot-steps, but only 2,900 of them produce a token: \u003cb>36% utilisation\u003c/b>. New requests queue until the whole batch finishes, so their time to first token balloons.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Continuous batching, introduced by the Orca system (OSDI 2022) and popularised by vLLM, schedules at every iteration:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>step:    1  2  3 ... 50 51 52 ... 80 81 ...\nslot 1:  A  A  A ... A  D  D  ... D  D  ...   D starts the step after A finishes\nslot 2:  B  B  B ... B  B  B  ... B  E  ...   E starts the step after B finishes\nslot 3:  C  C  C ... C  C  C  ... C  C  ...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Finished sequences leave immediately, waiting ones join immediately, and the GPU stays busy. Anyscale's benchmarks measured 8 times the throughput of naive batching from continuous batching alone, and up to 23 times with vLLM's memory optimisations, while also reducing median latency.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mixing prefill into the batch: chunked prefill\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A new request needs a prefill pass over its whole prompt, which is heavy compute. Run as one block, a 30,000-token prefill stalls every decoding user for a noticeable fraction of a second. \u003cb>Chunked prefill\u003c/b> (from the Sarathi-Serve work) splits long prompts into pieces that fit a per-step token budget and mixes them into steps alongside the decodes. In vLLM's current engine it is on by default and decodes are scheduled first. The budget is \u003ccode>--max-num-batched-tokens\u003c/code>: smaller values (vLLM's guide gives 2,048 as an example) protect inter-token latency; larger values improve TTFT and throughput, and the guide recommends more than 8,192 for throughput on large GPUs. \u003ccode>--max-num-seqs\u003c/code> caps how many sequences run per step.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Load-test it and plot the curve\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start a server (\u003ccode>vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B --max-model-len 8192\u003c/code>), then sweep concurrency with vLLM's benchmark client:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>for c in 1 4 16 64 128; do\n  n=$(( c * 10 )); [ \"$n\" -lt 40 ] &amp;&amp; n=40\n  vllm bench serve --model Qwen/Qwen3-8B --dataset-name random \\\n    --random-input-len 1000 --random-output-len 200 --ignore-eos \\\n    --num-prompts \"$n\" --max-concurrency \"$c\" --seed \"$c\" \\\n    --percentile-metrics ttft,tpot,e2el --metric-percentiles 50,95 \\\n    --save-result --result-filename \"bench_c$c.json\"\ndone\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ncs = [1, 4, 16, 64, 128]\nruns = [json.load(open(f\"bench_c{c}.json\")) for c in cs]\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 4))\nax[0].plot(cs, [r[\"output_throughput\"] for r in runs], marker=\"o\")\nax[0].set(xscale=\"log\", xlabel=\"concurrency\", ylabel=\"output tokens/s\", title=\"Throughput\")\nax[1].plot(cs, [r[\"p95_ttft_ms\"] for r in runs], marker=\"o\", label=\"p95 TTFT\")\nax[1].plot(cs, [r[\"p95_tpot_ms\"] for r in runs], marker=\"o\", label=\"p95 TPOT\")\nax[1].set(xscale=\"log\", yscale=\"log\", xlabel=\"concurrency\", ylabel=\"ms\", title=\"Latency\")\nax[1].legend()\nplt.savefig(\"throughput_vs_concurrency.png\", dpi=120)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--ignore-eos\u003c/code> makes every request produce exactly 200 tokens, and a different \u003ccode>--seed\u003c/code> per run stops prompts from an earlier run being served from the prefix cache, which the vLLM docs warn inflates results.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you load-test vLLM and plot throughput against concurrency, and what shape should you expect?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start the server, then run \u003ccode>vllm bench serve\u003c/code> with a fixed workload (for example 1,000 input and 200 output tokens with \u003ccode>--ignore-eos\u003c/code>) at concurrency 1, 4, 16, 64 and 128 using \u003ccode>--max-concurrency\u003c/code>, saving JSON results with a different seed per run. Plot \u003ccode>output_throughput\u003c/code> against concurrency, and p95 TTFT and TPOT on a second chart. Throughput rises steeply then flattens as the GPU saturates; TPOT rises slowly with batch size; TTFT jumps once requests start queueing or the KV cache fills and the engine preempts. The useful capacity is the highest concurrency that still meets your latency targets.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the static batching example, why is utilisation only 36%?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The batch lasts as long as its longest sequence (1,000 steps), but the other seven finish early and their slots sit idle; only 2,900 of the 8,000 slot-steps produce tokens. Continuous batching refills those slots immediately.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>--max-num-batched-tokens\u003c/code> trade off?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is the per-step token budget shared by decodes and prefill chunks. A small budget keeps steps short, protecting inter-token latency for users already streaming; a large budget processes prompts faster, improving TTFT and throughput, at the risk of longer, spikier steps.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:serving/prefix-caching-and-radixattention-in-servers":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Many requests start with the same tokens: a system prompt, tool definitions, few-shot examples, a document, earlier turns of a chat. \u003cb>Prefix caching\u003c/b> keeps the KV cache of finished requests and reuses it when a new request begins with exactly the same tokens, so that part of the prefill is skipped. vLLM finds matches by hashing fixed-size blocks; SGLang keeps a \u003cb>radix tree\u003c/b> of token sequences. It is the same mechanism that sits under provider prompt caching.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>vLLM: chains of block hashes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each \u003cb>full\u003c/b> block of the KV cache (for example 16 tokens) gets a hash computed from three things: the parent block's hash, the token ids in this block, and extras such as a LoRA adapter id, image hashes or a cache salt. Because each hash includes its parent, a block's hash identifies the entire prefix up to and including it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Block 1: hash(None,    \"You are a helpful support\")   -&gt; h1\nBlock 2: hash(h1,      \" assistant for Acme. Answer\")  -&gt; h2\nBlock 3: hash(h2,      \" only from the sources ...\")   -&gt; h3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new request hashes its prompt block by block and looks each hash up until the first miss; all blocks before the miss are reused. When a request finishes, its blocks are not erased; they stay cached until the memory is needed, then the least recently used are evicted. Only full blocks are cached. In vLLM's current engine this is on by default, and the default hash is SHA-256 (collision-resistant, chosen from v0.11). The documentation is explicit about the limit: prefix caching only shortens prefill; it does nothing for long generations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>SGLang: RadixAttention\u003c/h4>\u003cp>SGLang stores cached token sequences and their KV in a \u003cb>radix tree\u003c/b>, a compressed prefix tree whose edges hold runs of tokens. A new request walks the tree to find its longest cached prefix; branches split where conversations diverge; least recently used leaves are evicted first. SGLang can also schedule waiting requests by longest shared prefix (\u003ccode>--schedule-policy lpm\u003c/code>) to raise hit rates. The SGLang paper reports up to 6.4 times higher throughput than other systems on workloads with lots of sharing, such as agents, few-shot benchmarks and multi-turn chat. Across replicas, the SGLang Model Gateway's default \u003ccode>cache_aware\u003c/code> policy sends each request to the worker most likely to already hold its prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it buys\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A support bot with a 4,000-token system prompt and tool list, plus 500 new tokens per request, on Qwen3-8B (prefill costs about 2 × parameters FLOPs per token):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>without reuse: 2 × 8.19e9 × 4,500 tokens ≈ 7.4e13 FLOPs ≈ 0.15 s on an H100 at 50% utilisation\nwith reuse:    2 × 8.19e9 ×   500 tokens ≈ 8.2e12 FLOPs ≈ 0.017 s\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Nine times less prefill work per request: lower TTFT and more GPU time left for decoding. Multi-turn chat benefits the same way, since turn N re-sends turns 1 to N − 1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Designing for hits\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The rules are the same as for provider prompt caching (Billing module), because the mechanism is the same:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stable first, variable last.\u003c/b> No timestamps, request ids or user names at the top of the prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Identical tokens.\u003c/b> Same chat template, same tool order (sort tools), same whitespace. One changed token early on invalidates everything after it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Append-only history.\u003c/b> Editing or summarising early turns breaks the prefix from that point on.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep a conversation on one replica.\u003c/b> With round-robin across 8 replicas, each request has only a 1 in 8 chance of landing where its prefix is warm. Use sticky sessions or cache-aware routing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Leave memory headroom.\u003c/b> Under pressure, cached blocks are evicted first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure the hit rate\u003c/b>: vLLM exposes \u003ccode>vllm:prefix_cache_hits\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>vllm:prefix_cache_queries\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>/metrics\u003c/code>; their ratio is the token hit rate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Security: a timing side channel\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A shared cache can leak information between tenants. If a request containing a guess at another tenant's prompt comes back with a noticeably faster TTFT, the guess was probably cached. vLLM supports a per-request \u003ccode>cache_salt\u003c/code>, mixed into the first block's hash, so only requests with the same salt can share blocks. In multi-tenant deployments, salt per tenant, and keep a cryptographic hash rather than a fast non-cryptographic one, which the docs warn could cause collisions or leaks.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How does vLLM decide that part of a new prompt is already cached?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It splits the prompt into fixed-size blocks and computes each block's hash from the parent block's hash, the block's token ids and extras such as LoRA id or cache salt. It looks the hashes up in order and reuses every block up to the first miss; because each hash chains its parent, a match guarantees the whole prefix is identical. Partial blocks are never reused.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You run 8 replicas of a chat model behind a round-robin load balancer and see a low prefix-cache hit rate. Why, and what would you change?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each conversation's next turn lands on a random replica, so its cached prefix is usually on a different GPU. Use sticky routing by conversation or user, or a cache-aware router such as SGLang Model Gateway's \u003ccode>cache_aware\u003c/code> policy, so requests go where their prefix is already cached.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why would a multi-tenant deployment use a per-tenant cache salt?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Without it, one tenant could probe for another tenant's prompt contents by measuring whether a guessed prefix returns faster (a cache hit). Salting the hash per tenant confines reuse to requests within the same tenant.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:speculative-decoding":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In decode, the big model spends each step reading all its weights to produce one token, and it could check several tokens for nearly the same cost. \u003cb>Speculative decoding\u003c/b> lets a cheap drafter guess the next few tokens, then the big model verifies them all in one pass and keeps the longest correct run. A rejection-sampling rule makes the output distribution exactly the big model's, so it is a pure latency optimisation: same answers, fewer slow steps.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The algorithm\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>A \u003cb>drafter\u003c/b> proposes γ tokens (say 4).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The \u003cb>target\u003c/b> model runs one forward pass over the context plus the γ draft tokens, producing its own probabilities at every position.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Draft tokens are checked left to right. With greedy decoding a token is accepted if it matches the target's choice; with sampling it is accepted with probability min(1, p_target / p_draft). At the first rejection the target samples a corrected token from an adjusted distribution and the rest are discarded. If all γ pass, the target adds one bonus token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every target pass yields between 1 and γ + 1 tokens.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>How much faster? The maths\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let α be the acceptance rate (the chance a draft token is accepted) and c the drafter's cost relative to one target pass. From the original paper:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>expected tokens per target pass = (1 - α^(γ+1)) / (1 - α)\nspeed-up ≈ expected tokens per pass / (γ × c + 1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With γ = 4 and a drafter costing 5% of the target (c = 0.05):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Acceptance rate α\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens per target pass\u003c/th>\u003cth>Speed-up\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0.6\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.31\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.9x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0.7\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.77\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.3x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0.8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3.36\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.8x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0.9\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3.4x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Acceptance dominates. Proposing more tokens only pays when acceptance is high, and acceptance depends on the content: boilerplate code, JSON and quoting from the context are easy to predict; creative text is not. The original paper reported 2 to 3 times faster decoding on T5-XXL with identical outputs, and DeepMind's parallel work 2 to 2.5 times on the 70B Chinchilla.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Kinds of drafters\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\u003cth>Drafter\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strengths\u003c/th>\u003cth>Costs\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Draft model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A small model from the same family (same tokenizer)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing to train if one exists\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Extra memory; must match tokenizer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>N-gram (prompt lookup), suffix decoding\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Copies continuations already present in the prompt or output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Free; strong for code edits, RAG and extraction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Little gain on open-ended text\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Medusa\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Extra decoding heads on the target model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No separate model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Needs training; paper reports 2.2 to 3.6x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>EAGLE, EAGLE-3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A small head that reads the target's own hidden states\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Highest acceptance; EAGLE-3 reports up to 6.5x and about 1.4x over EAGLE-2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A trained head per target model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MTP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model's built-in multi-token-prediction layers (for example DeepSeek-V3 and V4)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing extra to load\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only models trained with MTP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>SGLang's documentation gives a concrete example: Llama 3.1 8B on one H100 on MT-Bench generated about 158 tokens per second without speculation, 244 with EAGLE-2 and 373 with EAGLE-3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Enabling it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>vLLM, with a published EAGLE-3 head for Qwen3-8B:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B \\\n  --speculative-config '{\"method\": \"eagle3\", \"model\": \"RedHatAI/Qwen3-8B-speculator.eagle3\", \"num_speculative_tokens\": 3}'\n\n# no extra model: n-gram prompt lookup, good for RAG and code editing\nvllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B \\\n  --speculative-config '{\"method\": \"ngram\", \"num_speculative_tokens\": 4, \"prompt_lookup_min\": 2, \"prompt_lookup_max\": 5}'\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>SGLang, following its documented EAGLE-3 example:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python -m sglang.launch_server --model-path meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \\\n  --speculative-algorithm EAGLE3 \\\n  --speculative-draft-model-path jamesliu1/sglang-EAGLE3-Llama-3.1-Instruct-8B \\\n  --speculative-num-steps 3 --speculative-eagle-topk 4 --speculative-num-draft-tokens 16 \\\n  --mem-fraction-static 0.7\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Measuring it, and when it does not help\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Run the same \u003ccode>vllm bench serve\u003c/code> workload with and without speculation at concurrency 1 and at your production concurrency, and compare median and p95 TPOT (time per output token). Check the acceptance rate from the server's \u003ccode>/metrics\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>vllm:spec_decode_\u003c/code> counters) or logs. Use realistic prompts: random-token benchmarks give unrealistically low acceptance.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Speculation trades spare compute for latency. At low load the GPU has idle compute, so verification is nearly free. At high load the GPU is already compute-bound by large batches, verification of rejected tokens is wasted work, and gains shrink or disappear; vLLM describes the feature as reducing inter-token latency under medium-to-low load. Both vLLM and SGLang now offer dynamic or adaptive speculation, which adjusts how much to draft as load and acceptance change.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you enable speculative decoding and measure the latency change?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Restart the server with a drafter, for example \u003ccode>vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B --speculative-config '{\"method\": \"eagle3\", \"model\": \"RedHatAI/Qwen3-8B-speculator.eagle3\", \"num_speculative_tokens\": 3}'\u003c/code> (or the n-gram method if no head exists). Run an identical \u003ccode>vllm bench serve\u003c/code> workload with realistic prompts against the server with and without speculation, at concurrency 1 and at production concurrency, and compare median and p95 TPOT and end-to-end latency, plus output throughput. Read the acceptance rate from \u003ccode>/metrics\u003c/code>. Expect a clear TPOT improvement at low concurrency and a smaller one, or none, at high concurrency.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With γ = 4 and an acceptance rate of 0.8, how many tokens does each target pass produce on average, and why is that not the full speed-up?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(1 − 0.8^5) / (1 − 0.8) ≈ 3.36 tokens per pass. The drafter's own work and the larger verification pass cost time, so with a drafter at 5% of the target's cost the speed-up is about 3.36 / 1.2 ≈ 2.8 times.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does speculative decoding not change the model's output distribution?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Draft tokens are accepted with probability min(1, p_target / p_draft) and rejections are resampled from a corrected distribution, which provably yields samples from exactly the target model's distribution; with greedy decoding, only tokens matching the target's own choice are kept.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:flashattention":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Standard attention writes the full N × N matrix of scores to the GPU's large but slow memory (HBM) and reads it back, so for long sequences the time goes on moving data, not on arithmetic. \u003cb>FlashAttention\u003c/b> computes attention in tiles that fit in the small, fast on-chip SRAM, using an \u003cb>online softmax\u003c/b> so the N × N matrix is never stored. The result is exact, memory grows linearly instead of quadratically, and long-context attention runs several times faster.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The GPU memory hierarchy\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The FlashAttention paper describes an A100: 40 to 80 GB of HBM at 1.5 to 2.0 TB/s, and 192 KB of on-chip SRAM in each of its 108 streaming multiprocessors (about 20 MB in total) at roughly 19 TB/s. SRAM is an order of magnitude faster but thousands of times smaller. Any operation that does little arithmetic per byte it touches (softmax, masking, scaling) is limited by memory traffic, so the goal is to touch HBM as rarely as possible.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How much traffic standard attention causes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For one head with N = 8,192 tokens, the score matrix S = QKᵀ has 8,192 × 8,192 ≈ 67 million entries, 134 MB in 16-bit. Standard attention writes S to HBM, reads it for the softmax, writes the probabilities P, then reads P again to multiply by V: about 4 × 134 MB ≈ 0.54 GB of traffic for a single head in a single layer. For a model with 32 heads and 36 layers that is about 620 GB per 8k-token prefill, around 0.18 s on an H100 spent only shuffling scores. The actual inputs and outputs, Q, K, V and O, are about 2 MB each per head.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Tiling and the online softmax\u003c/h4>\u003cp>FlashAttention loads a block of Q rows into SRAM, then streams blocks of K and V past it. For each new block it computes that block's scores, updates a running softmax, and accumulates the output, all in SRAM. Only Q, K, V and the final O touch HBM.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The trick that makes this possible is that softmax can be computed incrementally. Keep a running maximum m, a running sum l of exponentials, and a running weighted sum of values. When a new block raises the maximum, rescale what you had:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import numpy as np\n\ndef online_softmax_attention(scores, values, block=2):\n    \"\"\"Softmax(scores) @ values, one block at a time, never holding all weights.\"\"\"\n    m, l, acc = -np.inf, 0.0, np.zeros(values.shape[1])\n    for i in range(0, len(scores), block):\n        s, v = scores[i:i + block], values[i:i + block]\n        m_new = max(m, s.max())\n        scale = np.exp(m - m_new)          # rescale everything accumulated so far\n        p = np.exp(s - m_new)\n        l = l * scale + p.sum()\n        acc = acc * scale + p @ v\n        m = m_new\n    return acc / l                         # identical to the standard computation\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A tiny check: scores [1, 3] then [2, 0]. After the first block m = 3 and l = e^(1−3) + e^0 = 1.135. The second block's maximum is 2, so m stays 3 and l becomes 1.135 + e^(2−3) + e^(0−3) = 1.553, exactly the denominator of the full softmax computed with maximum 3. For training, the backward pass recomputes attention tiles instead of storing the N × N matrix, trading cheap arithmetic for expensive memory.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Versions and results\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>FlashAttention (2022)\u003c/b>: 3 times faster GPT-2 training at sequence length 1K and 2.4 times on long-range benchmarks, with linear memory; it made much longer training contexts practical.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>FlashAttention-2 (2023)\u003c/b>: about 2 times faster again through better work partitioning, reaching 50 to 73% of the A100's theoretical peak.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>FlashAttention-3 (2024)\u003c/b>: uses Hopper features (asynchronous tensor cores and memory movement, FP8), 1.5 to 2.0 times faster than version 2 on H100, up to 740 TFLOP/s in FP16 (75% utilisation) and close to 1.2 PFLOP/s in FP8.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Decode needs different kernels, because each new token has a single query row against a long KV cache; split-KV variants and libraries such as FlashInfer handle this case, and serving engines combine them with paged KV caches.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You rarely call FlashAttention directly. vLLM and SGLang choose an attention backend automatically (vLLM accepts \u003ccode>--attention-backend FLASH_ATTN\u003c/code> to force one), llama.cpp has \u003ccode>-fa\u003c/code>, and Ollama turns it on automatically where the hardware supports it; Ollama's KV-cache quantisation even requires it. What you should know is what it solves and what it does not: it removes the N × N score matrix, the memory and bandwidth problem of attention computation, but not the KV cache, which still grows with every token and is handled by paging, quantisation and architecture choices such as grouped-query attention.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain why tiling attention reduces memory traffic.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Standard attention materialises the N × N score and probability matrices in HBM, writing and reading them several times, which for long sequences costs far more time than the arithmetic. Tiling loads blocks of Q, K and V into fast on-chip SRAM and computes scores, softmax and the weighted sum for each block there, using an online softmax that rescales running maxima and sums as new blocks arrive. The N × N matrix never goes to HBM, so traffic falls from quadratic in N to roughly the size of Q, K, V and O, and memory use becomes linear.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Does FlashAttention change the model's outputs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No. It is an exact algorithm that reorders the same computation; results match standard attention up to normal floating-point rounding differences. Approximate methods such as sparse attention are a different thing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A long-context deployment runs out of GPU memory. Will a faster FlashAttention version fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Probably not. FlashAttention removes the temporary N × N attention matrices, which current engines already avoid, but the persistent KV cache still grows with every token of every request. You need a smaller maximum context, an FP8 KV cache, fewer concurrent requests, more GPUs, or a model with a more compact cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:serving/parallelism-tensor-pipeline-and-expert-parallel":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>When one GPU is not enough, you split the work. \u003cb>Data parallelism\u003c/b> runs independent copies for more throughput. \u003cb>Tensor parallelism\u003c/b> splits every layer's matrices across GPUs, which fits bigger models and cuts latency but needs very fast links. \u003cb>Pipeline parallelism\u003c/b> gives each GPU a slice of the layers, which spans nodes but adds latency. \u003cb>Expert parallelism\u003c/b> spreads a mixture-of-experts model's experts across GPUs. Real deployments combine them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The four kinds\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\u003cth>What is split\u003c/th>\u003cth>Communication\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use it when\u003c/th>\u003cth>vLLM flag\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data (DP)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing: full replicas\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None between replicas; a load balancer in front\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model fits; you need more throughput\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--data-parallel-size\u003c/code>, or separate servers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tensor (TP)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every weight matrix, across GPUs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two all-reduces per layer, for every token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model or its KV cache does not fit one GPU but fits one node; lower latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--tensor-parallel-size\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pipeline (PP)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Groups of layers (stages)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Activations passed stage to stage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model does not fit one node; GPUs lack NVLink; uneven splits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--pipeline-parallel-size\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Expert (EP)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The experts of MoE layers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All-to-all exchange of tokens per MoE layer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Large MoE models at scale\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--enable-expert-parallel\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Tensor parallelism in one picture\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Megatron-LM's scheme splits a transformer's MLP so that each GPU does half of each matrix multiply:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>MLP:  Y = act(X · A) · B          X: activations, A and B: weight matrices\n\nGPU 0:  X · A[:, first half]  -&gt; act -&gt; · B[first half, :]   -&gt; partial Y0 \\\n                                                                            all-reduce: Y = Y0 + Y1\nGPU 1:  X · A[:, second half] -&gt; act -&gt; · B[second half, :]  -&gt; partial Y1 /\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Attention is split the same way, by heads. That costs two all-reduces per layer in the forward pass, for every decode step, so interconnect bandwidth matters enormously: an H100 SXM's NVLink gives 900 GB/s per GPU, against 128 GB/s for PCIe Gen5. vLLM's guidance is to keep tensor parallelism inside one node, and on GPUs without NVLink (such as L40S) to prefer pipeline parallelism.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: Qwen3-32B on 80 GB GPUs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Qwen3-32B in BF16 is 65.5 GB of weights, with 256 KiB of KV per token (64 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Layout\u003c/th>\u003cth>Weights per GPU\u003c/th>\u003cth>KV pool per GPU\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens the system holds\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1 GPU, BF16\u003c/td>\u003ctd>65.5 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 5 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 19,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fits, but barely serves anyone\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1 GPU, FP8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32.8 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 38 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 144,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often the cheapest good answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 GPUs, BF16, TP=2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32.8 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 38 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 288,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each GPU stores half of every token's KV (4 of the 8 KV heads)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 GPUs, FP8, DP=2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32.8 GB each\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 38 GB each\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 × 144,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two independent replicas\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>TP=2 also lowers per-token latency, since each GPU reads only half the weights per step, though not by a full factor of two because of the all-reduces. DP=2 gives more total throughput and survives one GPU failing. Quantising first often removes the need for a second GPU, which is why \"try FP8 before TP\" is a common rule.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mixture-of-experts and expert parallelism\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In an MoE model each token is routed to a few experts per layer: gpt-oss-120b has 128 experts and uses 4 per token (5.1B active of 117B parameters); DeepSeek-V4-Pro reports 1.6T total and 49B active. Memory is dominated by experts that each token rarely touches. Expert parallelism places different experts on different GPUs and ships each token's hidden state to the GPUs holding its chosen experts and back (an \u003cb>all-to-all\u003c/b> exchange per MoE layer). Two practical issues:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Load imbalance\u003c/b>: popular experts overload their GPU. Expert-parallel load balancers duplicate hot experts (SGLang exposes options such as \u003ccode>--ep-num-redundant-experts\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Attention is still dense\u003c/b>, so large deployments run attention data-parallel and the MoE layers expert-parallel; vLLM's documentation notes that expert parallelism is typically coupled with data parallelism, on one node or across many.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Choosing a layout\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The vLLM documentation's decision list, extended:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fits on one GPU with room for KV at your concurrency\u003c/b>: use one GPU per replica; scale out with data parallelism behind a load balancer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Does not fit, or too little KV\u003c/b>: first try FP8 weights or KV; then tensor parallelism within a node (2, 4 or 8 GPUs).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Does not fit in one node\u003c/b>: tensor parallelism equal to GPUs per node, pipeline parallelism equal to the number of nodes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Large MoE\u003c/b>: expert parallelism for the MoE layers, typically with data-parallel attention.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>After starting, read the log lines \u003ccode>GPU KV cache size\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Maximum concurrency\u003c/code> to confirm the layout gives the capacity you planned. Very long contexts add a further option, context parallelism, which splits a single sequence's KV across GPUs.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-32B --tensor-parallel-size 2                    # one node, 2 GPUs\nvllm serve &lt;model&gt; --tensor-parallel-size 8 --pipeline-parallel-size 2 # 2 nodes of 8 GPUs\nvllm serve &lt;moe-model&gt; --data-parallel-size 8 --enable-expert-parallel # DP attention, EP experts\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need to serve Qwen3-32B with plenty of concurrent users on 80 GB H100s. What layouts would you consider, and how would you decide?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>BF16 on one GPU leaves only about 5 GB for KV, so it is not viable. Options: FP8 weights on one GPU (about 144,000 KV tokens), scaled out with data-parallel replicas; or BF16 with tensor parallelism 2 (about 288,000 tokens per pair and lower per-token latency). Decide by running your eval on the FP8 model and load-testing both layouts for throughput per GPU at your latency target; FP8 with replicas is usually cheaper per token if quality holds.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should tensor parallelism stay within a node with NVLink?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It performs two all-reduces per layer on every decode step, moving activations between GPUs constantly. NVLink offers about 900 GB/s per GPU on H100 SXM against 128 GB/s for PCIe Gen5 and far less between nodes, so on slow links communication dominates and latency suffers; pipeline parallelism, which only passes activations between stages, tolerates slower links.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does expert parallelism split, and what new communication does it add?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It places the experts of each MoE layer on different GPUs, so each GPU stores only some experts. Each token's hidden state must be sent to the GPUs holding its selected experts and the results sent back: an all-to-all exchange in every MoE layer, which also creates load-balancing problems when some experts are more popular.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:serving/disaggregated-prefill-and-decode":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prefill is a short burst of heavy compute; decode is a long, memory-bound trickle. On the same GPUs they interfere: a big prefill stalls everyone's next token. \u003cb>Disaggregation\u003c/b> runs prefill on one pool of GPUs and decode on another and ships the KV cache between them, so time to first token and inter-token latency can be tuned independently. It improves tail latency and goodput under latency targets at scale; it does not by itself raise raw throughput, and it adds a fast network to the critical path.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The interference problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A GPU is decoding for 64 users at about 10 ms per step. A request with a 32,768-token prompt arrives. Prefilling it on Qwen3-8B costs about 2 × 8.19e9 × 32,768 ≈ 5.4e14 FLOPs, roughly 1.1 s on an H100 at 50% utilisation. Run in one piece, every one of the 64 streams freezes for over a second: a huge inter-token latency spike at p99. Chunked prefill (the continuous batching lesson) spreads that work across many steps, which helps, but vLLM's documentation notes that the right chunk size is hard to find in practice, and every chunk still slows the steps it shares.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How disaggregation works\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Router\u003c/b>\u003csmall>picks a prefill worker\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Prefill pool\u003c/b>\u003csmall>compute-heavy, builds the KV cache\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>KV transfer\u003c/b>\u003csmall>NVLink or RDMA, via NIXL, Mooncake or LMCache\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Decode pool\u003c/b>\u003csmall>memory-bound, streams tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The KV cache must cross the network. For Qwen3-8B (144 KiB per token), an 8,000-token prompt produces about 1.18 GB of KV:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>1.18 GB over 400 Gb/s RDMA (50 GB/s)   ≈ 24 ms\n1.18 GB over 100 Gb/s      (12.5 GB/s) ≈ 94 ms\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>That transfer adds directly to TTFT, which is why disaggregated clusters use RDMA or NVLink fabrics and why models with compact KV caches (grouped-query attention, multi-head latent attention, sliding windows) disaggregate more easily.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it buys\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Independent tuning.\u003c/b> Prefill workers can use high tensor parallelism for fast TTFT while decode workers use cheaper layouts tuned for throughput at your TPOT target.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No interference.\u003c/b> Decode steps never wait behind prefills, so tail inter-token latency becomes stable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hardware matched to the phase.\u003c/b> Splitwise (Microsoft) reported 1.4 times the throughput at 20% lower cost, or 2.35 times the throughput at the same cost and power, by giving each phase suitable machines.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Goodput.\u003c/b> DistServe defines goodput as the request rate served within both TTFT and TPOT targets, and reported 7.4 times more requests, or 12.6 times tighter targets, than colocated systems while keeping over 90% of requests within their limits. Mooncake, the platform behind Moonshot's Kimi service, reported handling 75% more requests on real workloads with a disaggregated, KV-cache-centred design.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>vLLM's own documentation is blunt that disaggregated prefill does \u003cb>not\u003c/b> improve throughput. The win is controlling latency, which lets you run each GPU closer to its limit without breaking your targets. \u003ccode>vllm bench serve --goodput ttft:500 tpot:50\u003c/code> measures goodput directly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Where it lives\u003c/b>: NVIDIA Dynamo and llm-d (a Kubernetes-native stack that runs vLLM, SGLang and other engines across a cluster) orchestrate disaggregated serving; SGLang supports it with \u003ccode>--disaggregation-mode prefill\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--disaggregation-mode decode\u003c/code>; vLLM has it as an experimental feature with KV connectors such as \u003ccode>NixlConnector\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>MooncakeConnector\u003c/code> and LMCache, configured through \u003ccode>--kv-transfer-config\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When it is worth it\u003c/b>: many GPUs, long prompts (RAG, agents, code), strict latency targets and mixed traffic, which is the situation of large providers such as Moonshot with Kimi.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When it is not\u003c/b>: a deployment of one to a few GPUs. Chunked prefill and good scheduling get you most of the benefit without a transfer network and two pools to balance.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tune the ratio\u003c/b> of prefill to decode workers to your traffic: long prompts with short answers need more prefill capacity; chatty generation needs more decode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Related idea\u003c/b>: KV-cache tiers (LMCache, Mooncake's store) keep prefix caches in CPU memory or SSD and share them across instances, so a cached prefix survives eviction from one GPU.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Users complain about stutters in streamed answers whenever someone pastes a long document. Why does adding more GPUs to the same pool not reliably fix it, and what does?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each GPU still mixes prefills with decodes, so whichever GPU receives the long prompt stalls the streams it is serving. More GPUs make it rarer per GPU but do not remove it. Chunked prefill with a smaller per-step token budget reduces the stall; disaggregating prefill onto separate workers removes the interference entirely, at the cost of transferring the KV cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What must the network between prefill and decode workers carry, and how big is it for an 8,000-token prompt on Qwen3-8B?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The full KV cache of the prompt: 2 × 36 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 dims × 2 bytes ≈ 144 KiB per token, so about 1.18 GB, which takes roughly 24 ms over 400 Gb/s RDMA and adds to time to first token.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is goodput, and why is it a better target than raw throughput here?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Goodput is the rate of requests completed within your latency targets (for example TTFT under 500 ms and TPOT under 50 ms). Raw throughput counts requests that blew their targets; disaggregation is about serving more requests within the targets, which raw throughput does not measure.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:openai-compatible-serving":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>All the main engines speak the OpenAI chat API, so moving from a provider to self-hosting changes three strings in your code: base URL, key and model name. The engines differ in hardware support, speed on particular models, features (structured output, LoRA adapters, speculative decoding, disaggregation) and operational maturity. As of late 2026, vLLM and SGLang are the default choices for GPU serving, TensorRT-LLM for squeezing the most from NVIDIA hardware, and llama.cpp or Ollama for local and edge use.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The engines\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Engine\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strengths\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\u003cth>Start a server\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>vLLM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Broadest model and hardware support (NVIDIA, AMD, TPU, CPU and more); PagedAttention; prefix caching; many quantisation formats; speculative decoding; multi-LoRA; OpenAI and Anthropic-style endpoints; Prometheus metrics\u003c/td>\u003ctd>General-purpose GPU serving\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>vllm serve &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> (port 8000)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>SGLang\u003c/td>\u003ctd>RadixAttention prefix reuse; fast structured output; strong on large MoE and DeepSeek-style models; prefill/decode disaggregation; a cache-aware gateway\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High-throughput serving, agents and shared prefixes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sglang serve &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>python -m sglang.launch_server --model-path &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> (port 30000)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>TensorRT-LLM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>NVIDIA-optimised kernels behind a PyTorch-based API; integrates with Dynamo and Triton\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Maximum performance on NVIDIA at scale\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>trtllm-serve &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>llama.cpp server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GGUF; CPU, Apple and consumer GPUs; tiny footprint\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Local, edge, small internal services\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>llama-server -hf &lt;repo&gt;:&lt;quant&gt; --port 8080\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ollama\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Easiest local model management\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Developer laptops\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ollama serve\u003c/code> (port 11434)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hugging Face TGI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Was a leading server; now in maintenance mode, its maintainers recommend vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp or MLX\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Existing deployments only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Migrate\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Performance rankings between vLLM and SGLang change with every release and differ by model, so benchmark both on your model and traffic and pin the version you deploy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Point your client at your own server\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start vLLM with an API key and a friendly model name:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>uv pip install vllm --torch-backend=auto\nvllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B --served-model-name qwen3-8b --max-model-len 16384 \\\n  --reasoning-parser qwen3 --api-key \"$VLLM_API_KEY\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Then change only the client's base URL, key and model:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://gpu-box:8000/v1\", api_key=os.environ[\"VLLM_API_KEY\"])\nr = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"qwen3-8b\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Give me three test cases for a date parser.\"}],\n    extra_body={\"chat_template_kwargs\": {\"enable_thinking\": False}},   # Qwen3: skip thinking\n)\nprint(r.choices[0].message.content, r.usage)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>extra_body\u003c/code> passes engine-specific options through the OpenAI SDK. Here it switches off Qwen3's thinking mode, which vLLM documents as on by default for that family; \u003ccode>--reasoning-parser qwen3\u003c/code> separates any thinking into its own field instead of mixing it into the answer. Tool calling needs \u003ccode>--enable-auto-tool-choice\u003c/code> plus the right \u003ccode>--tool-call-parser\u003c/code> for the model, as listed on the model card or the engine's docs. SGLang works the same way at \u003ccode>http://host:30000/v1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Endpoints you get for free\u003c/h4>\u003cp>vLLM exposes \u003ccode>/v1/chat/completions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/v1/completions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/v1/responses\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/v1/embeddings\u003c/code> (for embedding models) and an Anthropic-style \u003ccode>/v1/messages\u003c/code>, plus \u003ccode>/v1/models\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/health\u003c/code> and Prometheus metrics at \u003ccode>/metrics\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Running it in production\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Behind your gateway\u003c/b>: the engine's \u003ccode>--api-key\u003c/code> is a single shared secret. Put the server on a private network and let the gateway (Reliability module) handle per-team keys, budgets and logging; register it under an alias so it can fail over to a hosted API.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Containers\u003c/b>: official images such as \u003ccode>vllm/vllm-openai\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>lmsysorg/sglang\u003c/code>; mount a model cache volume so restarts do not re-download tens of gigabytes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cold starts\u003c/b>: loading weights and compiling kernels can take minutes. Keep warm replicas, cache compiled artefacts (vLLM persists them under \u003ccode>~/.cache/vllm\u003c/code>) and pre-download kernels where supported (\u003ccode>vllm download-kernels\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Autoscale on queue depth, not CPU\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>vllm:num_requests_waiting\u003c/code> and KV-cache usage (\u003ccode>vllm:kv_cache_usage_perc\u003c/code>) say when you need another replica; CPU usage says almost nothing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Kubernetes\u003c/b>: KServe, llm-d, KubeAI and the vLLM production stack add routing, autoscaling and prefix-aware load balancing on top of the engines.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Upgrades\u003c/b>: engines release often and defaults change. Pin versions, and rerun your eval and load test before upgrading.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you point your client library at a self-hosted vLLM server?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start the server with \u003ccode>vllm serve &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> (optionally \u003ccode>--served-model-name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--api-key\u003c/code> and model-specific flags such as \u003ccode>--reasoning-parser\u003c/code>), then create the OpenAI client with \u003ccode>base_url=\"http://&lt;host&gt;:8000/v1\"\u003c/code>, the server's API key (any string if none is set) and the served model name. Nothing else in the code changes; engine-specific options go in \u003ccode>extra_body\u003c/code>. Check \u003ccode>/v1/models\u003c/code> to confirm the name and \u003ccode>/health\u003c/code> for readiness.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you pick SGLang over vLLM, and when llama.cpp over both?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>SGLang when your workload has heavy prefix sharing (agents, multi-turn, few-shot), needs very fast structured output, or runs large MoE models where it benchmarks faster, and only after measuring both on your model. llama.cpp (or Ollama) when you run on CPUs, Apple silicon or consumer GPUs, need GGUF quantisations, or serve one or a few users locally.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which metric should drive autoscaling of an inference server, and why not GPU utilisation or CPU?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Queue depth (requests waiting) together with KV-cache usage and latency against target. GPU utilisation can read high at moderate load because decoding keeps it busy, and CPU usage barely reflects inference load; waiting requests directly show that users are queueing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:self-hosting-versus-api-cost":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Self-hosting turns a per-token cost into a mostly fixed one: GPUs by the hour plus the people who run them. It wins on price when volume is high and steady and the API you would otherwise use is expensive. It usually loses against hosted APIs that sell the \u003cb>same open model\u003c/b>, because those providers pool many customers' traffic and keep their GPUs busy. Often the decision is made by data, control or latency requirements rather than price.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Step 1: your cost per token\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>self-hosted $ per million tokens = GPU $ per hour / (tokens per second × 3,600 × utilisation) × 1,000,000\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>On-demand H100s from specialist GPU clouds cost roughly $3 to $4 per GPU-hour in late 2026 (spot and long commitments differ). At $3.50:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Sustained output tokens/s per GPU\u003c/th>\u003cth>100% busy\u003c/th>\u003cth>50% busy\u003c/th>\u003cth>25% busy\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.94 per million\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$3.89\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$7.78\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1,500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.65\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$2.59\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.32\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.65\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.30\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two inputs dominate. \u003cb>Throughput\u003c/b> must come from your own load test at your latency target (the continuous batching lesson), not from a vendor's best-case number. \u003cb>Utilisation\u003c/b> is lower than people assume: you size for peak traffic, so over a day an interactive service may average well under half of its capacity.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Step 2: the fixed monthly cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Example assumptions, to be replaced with your own:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>GPUs:   2 × H100 (two, for redundancy) × $3.50 × 730 h  = $5,110\npeople: a quarter of an engineer at $200,000 a year    = $4,167\nother:  monitoring, storage, networking, load balancer  =   $500\ntotal:                                                 ≈ $9,777 per month, even with zero traffic\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Step 3: break-even per request\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Workload: Qwen3-32B in FP8 on H100s, 3,000 input and 500 output tokens per request. Suppose your load test shows 2 requests per second per GPU within your p95 target (about 1,000 output tokens per second; a bandwidth estimate puts the ceiling near 1,700 at batch 32). At 50% average utilisation one GPU serves about 2.6 million requests a month, so the GPU part costs about $0.00097 per request. Add GPUs in steps as volume grows, never fewer than two.\u003c/p>\u003cp>API prices per request for the same workload (illustrative late-2026 prices per million tokens): frontier closed model at $4 in / $20 out, $0.022; mid-tier closed model at $1 / $5, $0.0055; small closed model at $0.10 / $0.50, $0.00055; Qwen3-32B from a hosted provider at about $0.08 / $0.28, $0.00038.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Requests per month\u003c/th>\u003cth>Frontier API\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mid-tier API\u003c/th>\u003cth>Hosted Qwen3-32B\u003c/th>\u003cth>Self-hosted Qwen3-32B\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1 million\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$22,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$5,500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$380\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$9,777 (2 GPUs)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10 million\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$220,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$55,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$3,800\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$14,887 (4 GPUs)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>50 million\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1,100,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$275,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$19,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$55,767 (20 GPUs)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>break-even volume = fixed monthly cost / (API price per request - your marginal cost per request)\nversus frontier:  ≈ 500,000 requests a month (about 17,000 a day)\nversus mid-tier:  ≈ 1.8 million a month (about 60,000 a day)\nversus hosted Qwen3-32B or a small closed model: never; the GPU cost per request alone exceeds their price\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Read the comparison with care. Against frontier and mid-tier APIs it is only valid if the 32B model passes your eval for the task, which for the hardest tasks it often will not. API-side prompt caching and batch discounts lower the API column, and your own prefix caching can raise your throughput.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Step 4: the reasons that are not price\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For self-hosting:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Data may not leave your network (regulated data, contracts, air-gapped sites).\u003c/li>\u003cli>A fine-tuned model of your own (the Fine-tuning module), though many inference providers also serve fine-tunes and LoRA adapters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pinned weights that never change or get deprecated.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Latency control, co-location with your data, offline or edge use.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Against it: on-call duty, upgrades, GPU availability, security patching, capacity planning and the steady arrival of better models that you must evaluate and redeploy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Middle paths\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dedicated endpoints\u003c/b> from inference providers (Together, Fireworks, Baseten, Modal, Hugging Face Inference Endpoints, cloud model catalogues): you choose the model and hardware, they operate it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Serverless GPUs\u003c/b> (Modal, RunPod and others) bill by the second and scale to zero, which suits spiky or low traffic; Modal listed an H100 at about $0.0011 per second (about $3.95 an hour) in late 2026.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hybrid\u003c/b>: self-host the high-volume small model (router, classifier, embeddings) and call APIs for the hard steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you compute the break-even volume for self-hosting a model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Measure your throughput per GPU at your latency target with a load test, then compute your marginal cost per request: GPU price per hour divided by requests per GPU-hour at realistic utilisation. Add up the fixed monthly cost: the minimum GPUs for redundancy, engineering time, monitoring and storage. Break-even volume is the fixed cost divided by (API price per request minus your marginal cost per request); if your marginal cost exceeds the API price, there is no break-even. Finally check that your GPUs can actually serve that volume, and that the self-hosted model passes your eval for the task.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does self-hosting Qwen3-32B usually lose on price to a provider selling the same model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The provider pools traffic from many customers, runs at high utilisation with tuned engines and negotiated GPU prices, and spreads engineering over all of them. At the example's 50% utilisation, your GPU cost alone is about $0.00097 per request against their whole price of about $0.00038.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Using the first table, what does halving utilisation from 50% to 25% do to your cost per million tokens at 1,500 tokens per second?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It doubles it, from $1.30 to $2.59 per million output tokens, because you pay for the GPU-hours whether or not they produce tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:serving/serve-with-vllm-and-load-test":"\u003cp>You will turn the lessons into numbers on real hardware: predict the KV capacity, check it against vLLM's own report, then find the knee of the throughput curve and see prefix caching at work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A Linux machine with one NVIDIA GPU, rented by the hour from a GPU cloud or serverless platform if you have none (roughly $1 to $4 an hour; stop it when you finish).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Model choice by GPU memory: 40 GB or more, \u003ccode>Qwen/Qwen3-8B\u003c/code>; 24 GB, \u003ccode>Qwen/Qwen3-8B-FP8\u003c/code> (Ada generation or newer, such as L4, L40S or RTX 4090) or \u003ccode>Qwen/Qwen3-8B-AWQ\u003c/code> on older cards. Substitute your choice wherever the steps say \u003ccode>Qwen/Qwen3-8B\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Python 3.11 to 3.14 and \u003ccode>uv\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Install vLLM and the plotting library:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>uv venv --python 3.12 --seed &amp;&amp; source .venv/bin/activate\nuv pip install vllm --torch-backend=auto\nuv pip install matplotlib\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Predict before you run. With the \u003ccode>gpu_budget\u003c/code> helper from the memory lesson, compute how many KV tokens your GPU should hold at \u003ccode>util=0.92\u003c/code> and how many 8,192-token requests that allows. Write the numbers down.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Start the server and read its start-up log:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>export VLLM_API_KEY=local-test\nvllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B --max-model-len 8192 --reasoning-parser qwen3 --api-key \"$VLLM_API_KEY\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Find the lines \u003ccode>GPU KV cache size: ... tokens\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Maximum concurrency for 8,192 tokens per request: ...x\u003c/code> and compare them with step 2. Expect some difference in either direction: cards are sized in GiB (an \"80 GB\" H100 holds about 85 GB), and real overhead differs from the 3 GB guess.\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Smoke-test it from a second terminal, then from Python with streaming so you can see TTFT:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>curl -s http://localhost:8000/v1/models -H \"Authorization: Bearer $VLLM_API_KEY\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import os, time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=os.environ[\"VLLM_API_KEY\"])\nt0, first = time.perf_counter(), None\nstream = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\", stream=True,\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain the KV cache in 150 words.\"}],\n    extra_body={\"chat_template_kwargs\": {\"enable_thinking\": False}})\nfor chunk in stream:\n    if first is None and chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n        first = time.perf_counter() - t0\nprint(f\"TTFT {first * 1000:.0f} ms, total {time.perf_counter() - t0:.2f} s\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Sweep concurrency. \u003ccode>vllm bench serve\u003c/code> reads the key from \u003ccode>OPENAI_API_KEY\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>export OPENAI_API_KEY=$VLLM_API_KEY\nfor c in 1 4 16 64; do\n  n=$(( c * 10 )); [ \"$n\" -lt 40 ] &amp;&amp; n=40\n  vllm bench serve --model Qwen/Qwen3-8B --dataset-name random \\\n    --random-input-len 1000 --random-output-len 200 --ignore-eos \\\n    --num-prompts \"$n\" --max-concurrency \"$c\" --seed \"$c\" \\\n    --percentile-metrics ttft,tpot,e2el --metric-percentiles 50,95 \\\n    --save-result --result-filename \"bench_c$c.json\"\ndone\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>While the concurrency-64 run is going, watch the engine in another terminal:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>watch -n 1 'curl -s localhost:8000/metrics -H \"Authorization: Bearer $VLLM_API_KEY\" | grep -E \"^vllm:(num_requests_running|num_requests_waiting|kv_cache_usage_perc)\"'\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"7\">\u003cli>Plot throughput and latency against concurrency with the script from the continuous batching lesson (change \u003ccode>cs\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>[1, 4, 16, 64]\u003c/code>). Mark the highest concurrency whose p95 TPOT is under 33 ms (30 tokens per second per user) and whose p95 TTFT is under 1 s. That is this GPU's useful capacity for this workload.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"8\">\u003cli>See prefix caching. Run concurrency 16 with a shared 2,000-token prefix, then restart the server with \u003ccode>--disable-prefix-caching\u003c/code> and run it again:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>vllm bench serve --model Qwen/Qwen3-8B --dataset-name random --random-prefix-len 2000 \\\n  --random-input-len 500 --random-output-len 100 --num-prompts 160 --max-concurrency 16 \\\n  --seed 99 --percentile-metrics ttft --metric-percentiles 50,95\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"9\">\u003cli>Optional: restart with the EAGLE-3 config from the speculative decoding lesson, rerun concurrency 1 and 16, and compare median TPOT with your step 5 results.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"10\">\u003cli>Stop the server and shut the machine down.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>vLLM's reported KV capacity close to your prediction from step 2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Output throughput rising steeply from concurrency 1 to 16, then flattening; per-request TPOT creeping up as the batch grows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>TTFT staying low until the server saturates, then jumping as requests queue (\u003ccode>num_requests_waiting\u003c/code> above zero) or the KV cache fills.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the shared prefix, a clearly lower TTFT with prefix caching on, because only about 500 of 2,500 prompt tokens need prefill.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With speculative decoding, a TPOT improvement at concurrency 1 that shrinks at 16.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your p95 TTFT is fine at concurrency 16 but triples at 64, while throughput barely rises. What is happening and what would you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The server is saturated: the batch is as large as KV memory or compute allows, so extra requests wait in the queue (or get preempted), which shows up as TTFT. Throughput cannot rise further on this GPU. Cap concurrency per replica near 16 at the gateway and add replicas, or increase capacity per GPU with FP8 weights or KV cache, or a shorter maximum length.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the benchmark use a different seed for each run?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With the same seed, the random dataset repeats the same prompts, which may still be in the prefix cache from the previous run, making TTFT and throughput look better than real traffic would.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:self-hosted-model-endpoint":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a production-shaped endpoint for an open model and decide, with evidence, whether it should replace an API call. As an optional Atlas extension, self-host the model behind Atlas's \u003ccode>atlas-small\u003c/code> alias in the M14 gateway (the router or the simple-question answerer) and use the M11 eval suite; otherwise pick any task where you have 50 or more labelled examples. The deliverable is a short report that a team lead could act on.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One model served in two precisions with the same engine: BF16 and one quantised format (FP8 on Ada or newer GPUs, otherwise AWQ or GPTQ). SGLang is acceptable instead of vLLM.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reproducible deployment: a script or container definition (for example the \u003ccode>vllm/vllm-openai\u003c/code> image) with pinned engine version, model revision and flags.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Security basics: API key on the engine, the port not exposed publicly, traffic going through your gateway or a reverse proxy with TLS.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Observability: \u003ccode>/metrics\u003c/code> scraped by Prometheus or your tracing stack, with a dashboard of TTFT, inter-token latency, running and waiting requests, KV-cache usage and preemptions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A load test with production-shaped prompts (sampled lengths, real shared prefixes) at four concurrency levels, reporting throughput and p50 and p95 TTFT and TPOT.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Quality: the same eval run against both precisions and against the API model the endpoint would replace.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Cost: cost per million output tokens and per request at your measured throughput and a realistic utilisation, against the API price, with the break-even volume.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Plan\u003c/b> (1 h): pick the model and GPU with the memory maths; write down the expected KV capacity and maximum concurrency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deploy BF16\u003c/b> (1.5 h): scripted deployment, health check, API key, metrics, and a client or gateway calling it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Load test\u003c/b> (1.5 h): run the concurrency sweep, plot the curves, and identify the knee at your latency targets.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quantise\u003c/b> (1.5 h): deploy the quantised variant with the same flags, rerun the load test and the eval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tune\u003c/b> (1 to 2 h): try at least two of \u003ccode>--max-num-seqs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-num-batched-tokens\u003c/code>, FP8 KV cache, a shorter \u003ccode>--max-model-len\u003c/code>, prefix-friendly prompt ordering and speculative decoding; keep changes that help at your targets.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Decide\u003c/b> (1 h): cost model, break-even and recommendation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One command deploys the endpoint and another runs the full benchmark, producing JSON results and plots.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report has a table with, for each precision and the API: eval score, p95 TTFT and TPOT at the chosen concurrency, throughput per GPU, cost per million output tokens, and cost per request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report states the break-even volume against the API, with every assumption (GPU price, utilisation, engineering time) listed, and a recommendation: self-host, use a hosted endpoint for the same model, or stay on the API.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compare vLLM and SGLang on the same model and workload.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare two GPUs with tensor parallelism against two data-parallel replicas for throughput and latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Autoscale replicas on \u003ccode>vllm:num_requests_waiting\u003c/code> in Kubernetes and show the scaling under a ramping load test.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Serve a LoRA fine-tune from the Fine-tuning module alongside the base model and measure the overhead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the same model with llama.cpp on a laptop and add it to the table as an offline option.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Fix sampling settings (temperature 0, same \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>) for the eval so precision is the only variable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>--ignore-eos\u003c/code> and fixed output lengths in benchmarks so different runs do the same amount of work, then run one realistic test with natural stopping.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count utilisation honestly: average traffic over a day divided by the capacity you provisioned for peak.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
